 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the executive law, in relation to eligibility to receive\nawards from the crime victims board",
    "basePrintNo": "S8751",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8751-2017",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §631, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8751\n\nSPONSOR: SEPULVEDA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the executive law, in relation to eligibility to receive\nawards from the crime victims board\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThe bill amends section 631 of the executive law and would authorize the\ncrime victims board to accept other official documents in lieu of police\nreports for documentation of eligibility for compensation for rape,\nsexual assault, and child abuse and domestic violence.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nThe bill amends section 631 of the executive law and would authorize the\ncrime victims board to accept other official documents in lieu of police\nreports for documentation of eligibility for compensation for rape,\nsexual assault, and child abuse and domestic violence.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nStudies show that many victims need time to deal with the effects of the\ncrime and are not emotionally ready to go to the police immediately\nafter the crime has been committed. Additionally, many victims choose\nnot to report the crime to the police out of fear or shame. Victims\nshould be allowed to handle the aftermath of a crime in their own way\nand therefore should not,be prevented from asserting a claim and receiv-\ning compensation. Failing to file a police report should not be a bar to\ncompensation in these particular crimes.\n\nNew York State is not precluded under federal law or regulation from\nchanging the eligibility criteria as provided herein. In fact, in the\nrecently issued final program guidelines for the Federal Victim Compen-\nsation Grant Program, the Office for Victims of Crime in the Office of\nJustice Programs recognized the limitations of the law and endorsed the\nchange as proposed in this bill.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n02/01/13 referred to governmental operations\n01/08/14 referred to governmental operations\n01/20/15 A2723 referred to governmental operations\n01/06/16 A2723 referred to governmental operations\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nMinor increase in awards granted to newly eligible applicants.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect 30 days after the date on which it shall\nbecome a law.\n",
